          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A normed division algebra is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/not-necessarily+associative+algebra"} not-necessarily associative algebra
            over the real numbers that is:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unital"} unital
                (there is an element
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1a = a = a1
                for all
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebra"} division algebra
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                        %mtext i.e.
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mtext or
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi b
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big( \text{i.e.}\, (a \cdot b = 0) \Rightarrow (a = 0 \,\text{ or }\, b = 0) \big)
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+algebra"} normed algebra
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \|a b\| = \|a\| \|b\|
                for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
          %p
            It turns out (by
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurwitz%27+theorem"} Hurwitz' theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            there are precisely only four finite-dimensional normed division algebras up to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"}> real numbers
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"}> complex numbers
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"}> quaternions
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"}> octonions
                \.
          %p
            In this sense real normed division algebras may be thought of as a natural generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
          %p
            Moreover, if one regards the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
            with trivial
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-involution"} anti-involution
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley-Dickson+construction"} Cayley-Dickson construction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley-Dickson+construction"} Cayley-Dickson construction
            continues, next with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sedenions"} sedenions
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebras"} division algebras
          %p
            This classification of real normed division algebras is closely related to various other systems of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+exceptionalism"} exceptional
            structures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant+one+theorem"} Hopf invariant one theorem
                says that the only
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
                of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{2n-1}\to S^n
                whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant"} Hopf invariant
                is equal to 1 are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+constructions"} Hopf constructions
                on the four real normed division algebras, namely
              %ol
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+Hopf+fibration"} real Hopf fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+Hopf+fibration"} complex Hopf fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+Hopf+fibration"} quaternionic Hopf fibration
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    = succeed "." do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonionic+Hopf+fibration"} octonionic Hopf fibration
            %li
              %p
                Patterns related to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Majorana+spinors"} Majorana spinors
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+geometry"} spin geometry
                are intimately related to the four normed division algebras, and, induced by this, so is the classification of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
                in the form of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Poincar%C3%A9+Lie+algebras"} super Poincaré Lie algebras
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Minkowski+spacetimes"} super Minkowski spacetimes
                (which are built from these real spin representations). For more on this see at
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+division+algebras"} supersymmetry and division algebras
          %p
            (Moreover, apparently these two items are not unrelated, see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Equivariant+cohomology+of+M2%2FM5-branes"} here
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong normed division algebra
            is
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebra"} division algebra
            %li
              %p
                that is also a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach algebra
            %li
              %p
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo =
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x y\|} = {\|x\|} {\|y\|}
          %p
            While the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+algebra"} Banach algebra
            is in general only submultiplicative (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo ≤
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x y\|} \leq {\|x\|} {\|y\|}
            ), the norm in a normed division algebra must obey the stronger condition
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|x y\|} = {\|x\|} {\|y\|}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+value"} absolute value
            and often written
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|{-}|}
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|{-}\|}
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\|1\|} = 1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+space"} completeness
            condition of a Banach algebra, in fact (
            %a{:href => "#UrbanikWright60"}> UrbanikWright60
            ) the only examples have finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            and are therefore complete, hence Banach algebras.
          %p
            Any normed division algebras is in particular a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition+algebra"} composition algebra
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#Classification Classification
          %p
            Over the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} the
            only normed division algebra is the algebra of complex numbers themselves.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurwitz+theorem"} Hurwitz theorem
            says that over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            there are, up to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              , the algebra of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              , the algebra of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
              , the algebra of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
              , the algebra of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
          %p
            In fact these are also exactly the real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternative+algebra"} alternative
            division algebras:
          #ZornTheorem.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The only
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+algebras"} division algebras
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              which are also
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternative+algebras"} alternative algebras
              are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              themselves, the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
              and the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Zorn30"}> Zorn 30
            ).
          %p
            Each of these is produced from the previous one by the
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cayley%E2%80%93Dickson+construction"} Cayley–Dickson construction
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sedenions"} sedenions
            %em not
            form a division algebra.
          %p
            The Cayley–Dickson construction applies to an algebra with
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              is associative and commutative but has nontrivial involution,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
              is associative but noncommutative and with nontrivial involution,
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{O}
              is neither associative, commutative, nor with trivial involution.
          %p
            However, these algebras do all have some useful algebraic properties; in particular, they are all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternative+algebra"} alternative
            (a weak version of associativity). They are also all
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition+algebra"} composition algebra
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+field"} normed field
            is a commutative normed division algebra; it follows from the preceding that the only normed fields over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Tornheim52"} Tornheim 52
          %p
            It is in fact true that all
            %em unital
            normed division algebras over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            are already finite dimensional, by (
            %a{:href => "#UrbanikWright60"}> Urbanik-Wright 1960
            ) (the authors give a reference on a non-unital infinite-dimensional normed division algebra). Hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurwitz+theorem"} Hurwitz theorem
            together with
            %a{:href => "#UrbanikWright60"} Urbanik-Wright 1960
            says that the above four exhaust all real normed division algebras.
          %h3#Automorphisms Automorphisms
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/automorphism+groups"} automorphism groups
            of the real normed division algebras, as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+algebras"} normed algebras
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\mathbb{R}) = 1
                , the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial group
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\mathbb{C}) = \mathbb{Z}/2
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+order+2"} group of order 2
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugation"} complex conjugation
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℍ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi SO
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\mathbb{H}) = SO(3)
                , the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+orthogonal+group"} special orthogonal group
                acting via its canonical representation on the 3-dimensional space of imaginary quaternions;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝕆
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(\mathbb{O}) = G_2
                , the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exceptional+Lie+group"} exceptional Lie group
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G2"} G2
          %p
            Incidentally, there is a sense in which this sequence of groups continues, with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-group"} infinity-group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G3"} G3
            (the
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer-Wilkerson+H-space"} Dwyer-Wilkerson H-space
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=
                %th 0
                %th 1
                %th 2
                %th 3
                %th 4
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi DI
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} DI(n)=
